In article <3v8kgs$79a@usenet.pa.dec.com>, parris@ssag.enet.dec.com () writes:

>As I recall, the uVAX II hardware had some level of support for multiple CPUs,
>but there was no support in VMS to utilize this.  It was reportedly used for a
>research system called Andromeda, with up to 64 uVAX II CPUs.

The MP features were not in the MicroVAX II chip but on the KA630 CPU
board; Andromedia didn't use the KA630 board at all.  Incidentally,
although Andromeda may have been designed for up to 64 CPUs, the machines
built had 31 CPUs, whence the name M31.  Andromeda was essentially a 31-CPU
cluster-on-a-backplane.

