In article <DC0sI2.Lts@inter.NL.net>
sververd@solair1.inter.nl.net (Stephan Ververda) wrote:

> In article <3uibvf$nuf@mrnews.mro.dec.com>, Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com 
> says...
>>
>>PATHWORKS for OpenVMS gives OpenVMS the capability to become a LANman server,
>>but not a client. If you want VT *and* Windows NT users to access a file,
>>keep it on OpenVMS.
>>
>>With PATHWORKS, OpenVMS can become part of your Windows NT domain. We've got
>>one here, and it works fine. You can't tell whether your files are on
>>OpenVMS or another Windows NT server, and the files in the OpenVMS print
>>queues even show up in Print Manager.
>>
> 
> I'm also running a NT 3.5 Server with a PATHWORKS connection to the OpenVMS 
> Servers, the difference is that NT is running a own Domain, and the OpenVMS 
> servers are in a other domain.
> Whenever I want to browse the OpenVMS servers i get the following error:
> 
> An error occured in network provider Microsoft Windows Network. Error 6118: 
> The list of servers for this workgroup is not currently available.
> 
> This used to work just fine when I was working with NT 3.1/Pathworks V4.1 for 
> NT and Pathworks 4.? on the OpenVMS systems. Since I upgraded to NT 
> 3.5/Pathworks V4.1.B (at the same time the OpenVMS systems, i don't know their 
> version nr., upgraded to Pathworks 5.?) I'm getting this error....
> Do you know a solution for this??

The solution is to purchase the Pathworks License for your PC.  You really only need a 
FPA (File/Print Authority?) license since you are obviously using the Microsoft Windows
Network client software.  You also need somewhere on your site a CD with the 
Pathworks for Windows NT software.  It contains the License Requestor for NT that runs
as a service.  With this you can request either the full (Client + FPA) or just FPA license
from the server.  Digital does not license/charge for the Server software, they charge for
each user that connects to the server.

The error you are seeing is a Browse failure.  The VMS server will allow you to connect
to a Server and even a service but will not let you Browse without a license.  It is one 
of the number of things that require a license.  If you know the Server name and the
Service name you should be able to connect up without any problems, the Server will
just not provide you with a list to choose from..  (Don't ask me, I don't make up the rules..)

Hope this helps


