From: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
Subject: Re: Dual CPU uVaxII?
Date: 1995/07/21
Message-ID: <3uorfj$cim@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 106706872
distribution: world
references: <3ujn22$bvb@explorer.csc.com> <3unhat$qcp@mrnews.mro.dec.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <3ujn22$bvb@explorer.csc.com>, rmsmith@csc.com (Robert Smith)
writes:

>Is it possible to set up a uVax II (BA123) with two CPUs in the box/
>on the bus?

  Yes (as other posters have indicated), but the resulting configuration
  would be a multiprocessor -- not a symmetric multiprocessor (SMP) --
  system. For an OpenVMS SMP system to run, all processors must have access
  to all of the system's main memory -- and that's not possible with the
  KA630 multiprocessor.  (One *could* get this to run like a network on a
  (shared) Q-bus, but one would have to write a whole lot of non-trivial,
  hard-to-debug, and highly-privileged low-level VAX code...)

  The KA640 processor -- used in the MicroVAX 3300 and MicroVAX 3400 series
  systems -- is likely a much better choice for a home-grown multiprocessor
  system than the KA630, as it has four times (4MB) the on-board memory, and
  it has on-board network and I/O connections.  It's also far more commonly
  available as a "native" S-box module (in other words, it has the correct
  S-box handle and DMA grant wiring) -- all slots are Q22/CD in the S-box,
  which avoids the need for multiple BA23/BA123 enclosures to get enough
  Q22/CD slots for the processor module(s).

  These configurations are not supported by any Digital operating system I
  am aware of, and would be one heck of a lot of work to implement...  The
  technical details are present in the appropriate EK-KA6xx-xx CPU technical
  manual.  And while working inside the enclosure(s), one can fry one's
  hardware or one's self if not careful -- this project is not for folks
  inexperienced with Q-bus hardware or low-level system programming...

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------




