It's probably more correct to say that Windows NT/RISC anything does
not have a VGA driver.

Before purchasing hardware to plug into your system, be it, Intel,
Alpha, MIPS or PowerPC, please, please, please, check the latest
version of the Hardware Compatibility List to see what is supported on
what platform.

With PC type adapters, many Display cards and Controller cards use an
Extended BIOS mechanism to extend the systems BIOS. As the BIOS is
written in Intel code, typically, it is the Intel machines that are
capable of properly using this BIOS to intialize the cards. Granted,
once Windows NT is running, this BIOS is not used again, but it's the
initialization that's important.

I do not know what MIPS based machines nor PowerPC machines do about
this, but in some cases at Digital we have had to add code to the
systems Firmware to Initalize a card or so.

Astute Alpha users will note that current Alpha systems display a
message briefly about the BIOS emulator version Vx.xx on power up. This
is a constantly evolving mechanism to get around the above problem of
card initialization. I do not have any direct information on this and
so will not comment any further.

Regards, Richard Tomkins

