In article <DC2Lyn.6G3@ram.nl>, marc@ram.nl says...
>

>My VAX (4400 with 32Meg memory and running vms 5.5-2)
>is usings his pagefile(s) like hell. To solve this
>problem partially I created another pagefile on an
>unused disk. But the VAX is still using the 1st
>pagefile on the system disk for more then 50%
>of all the operations.
>

It's not clear from this whether you rebooted your Vax after adding the 
secondary pagefile or even stopped and restarted processes.

Basically the secondary pagefile should be larger than the primary (twice 
as big is very comfortable).  This is because the decision which pagefile 
to use is the one "estimated to have the most available free space, the 
one with the largest ratio of reservable blocks to total blocks." (Pg 461 
Goldenberg & Kenah).

You should also install it at system boot by using SYPAGSWPFILES.COM (in 
SYS$STARTUP).  This procedure is executed prior to SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM and 
therefore makes the secondary pagefile available to all processes started 
during system boot.  Incidentally the primary pagefile (always in 
SYS$SYSTEM) does not have to exist and you can use multiple secondary 
pagefiles to better balance the loading.

Adding a pagefile while the system is running usually requires some 
manual intervention to force processes to use the new pagefile.  
Typically this involves stopping and restarting processes - get users to 
log out and log in is good (if you can achieve this), but other actions 
like stopping all print queues and then restarting then will cause 
current processes to terminate and new one's to start - and they should 
use the new pagefile if the original ones are heavily loaded.

** This question would have been better placed in comp.os.vms - that's 
where the real technical gurus usually hang out and you would have got 
some fast responses !!

-- 
John McLean
VMS Technical Specialist
Croydon, Vic
Australia




