Hi, I'm curious to know if DEC is considering
using these 35ns DRAM's from RAMTRON.
They put the cache on the DRAM chip so many accesses would be
15ns or less.

Here is what RAMTRON claims for their chips:

4Mbx1 enhanced DRAM (EDRAM)
-  2Kbit SRAM cache memory for 15ns random reads within a page
-  interleave SRAM cache for 8ns burst read
-  fast 4Mbit DRAM array for 35ns access to any new page
-  write posting register for 15ns random writes and burst writes
   within a page (hit or miss)
-  256-byte wide DRAM to SRAM bus for 7.3G/s cache fill
-  On-chip cache hit/miss comparators maintains cache coherency 
   on writes
-  hidden precharge and refresh cycles

Here's a www page where EDRAM is described:

http://www.csn.net/ramtron/prod_info.html


I can see why DEC might not want to deal with RAMTRON,
they're an unknown company and don't have 16MB chips yet.
It's possible they couldn't guarantee DEC a supply.  
However, DEC might license the technology and be their
own supplier....

Regards,

PeterM



