The Inaugural Asia Pacific Distributed Solutions Event	
Hyatt Coolum, Sunshine Coast, Queensland, Australia 	
16-18 October 1995

The Inaugural Asia Pacific Distributed Solutions Event is a 
vendor-independent event initiated and hosted by the Cooperative Research 
Centre for Distributed Systems Technology (DSTC Pty Ltd).  DSTC is a 
non-profit research centre established and supported by the Australian 
Government�s Cooperative Research Centre�s Scheme.

The Event is an important contribution to the understanding of new 
technologies that managers will need to ensure their business remains 
competitive.  It brings together internationally respected industry 
analysts, researchers, users and vendors to help you gain insight and 
knowledge about new and evolving distributed technologies relevant to 
your business goals. The event is focused on presenting in-depth case 
studies to illustrate the business advantage that can be gained from 
distributed solutions. The theme for the event is Real Solutions for a 
Distributed World. This is not a talk fest. Each of the case studies has 
been selected to illustrate the very latest application of distributed 
technologies which  provide real solutions for business. 

Who Should Attend

The event is appropriate for information technology managers, systems 
analysts, and strategic business managers.

Programme

Keynote speakers (Ms Patricia Seybold from the Patricia Seybold Group and 
Mr Bob Hayward from the Gartner Group Asia Pacific Research Centre), an 
Expert Panel Discussion (including Ms Patricia Seybold and Mr Bob 
Hayward) - �Beyond DCE and CORBA�, seven case studies, expert panels, a 
World-Wide-Web and Internet seminar for business and a DCE/CORBA Users 
Group meeting.  

The Conference begins on Monday 16 October with Introductory Cocktails 
and a IBM Distributed Solutions Golf Classic on  Sunday 15 October.

Technologies at the Conference
	
Object middleware:	
Distributed Smalltalk
OMG CORBA	OSF DCE
ANSAware	ICL DAIS
Open Environment Entera	RPC mechanisms
Messaging Systems	Lotus Notes

Database middleware and transaction systems:	
Transactional Workflow
Multi-vendor database access	Oracle RDBMS
OLTP transactions	Tuxedo
Encina	

Standard Protocols:	
ATM
Z39.50	TCP/IP
X.25	Web/Internet protocols

Standard Services:
Resource Discovery	Distributed File Systems
X.500 Directory Services	Information Trading
Service Activation	Groupware services

Architectural Issues:
Legacy Application Integration	Three-tiered Architecture
Open Systems Architecture	Service Assurance
Business Process Re-engineering	

CASE STUDIES

Connections	Consultant Connections (Aust) Pty Ltd

Connections is an innovative 21st century Value Added Network business 
concept and software system.  This network product provides clients and 
members with electronic marketing, engagement services, groupware, 
membership accounting and a wide range of supporting services, including 
electronic data feeds to other networks and Internet access.

Connections is directed at the rapidly increasing workforce of 
independent consultants, contractors and service workers.  It provides a 
direct link between independents and their clients who outsource work and 
offers both parties a comprehensive and increasing range of services.  
This case study will look at the social and technology trends which 
inspired the creation of Connections and look at problems which must be 
faced when deploying technology that requires changes to work practices.

The software can be quickly adapted to meet the requirements of in-house 
consulting or contractor groups and current or emerging commercial, 
professional and social networks.  Written in Lotus Notes, � Connections 
is client/server based and runs on MS DOS, UNIX and WINDOWS NT based 
systems.  Client requirements are a PC, modem and a Lotus Notes run-time 
licence.

DEVETIR	Department of Employment, Vocational Education, Training and 
Industrial Relations

DEVETIR, a large Government Department in Queensland, Australia 
implemented in 1994 an Open Systems Architecture.  The primary focus of 
the Architecture is on forging a seamless web between the business needs 
of the Department and its Information Technology enablers through 
policies, plans and guidelines to ensure an integrated business 
perspective on IT management.  The principles of the Architecture, such 
as, business process re-design (BPR), leveraging of existing investment, 
elimination of duplication, and treating information as a corporate asset 
are applied to the Department�s I.T. initiatives.  BPR is currently being 
applied to selected business processes across a number of Departmental 
Business units.  The Department, like most organisations, is a 
distributed and mobile organisation.  The technology solutions required 
to support its business processes must also process this profile.

DSTC - A Distributed Environment Supporting Multi-Site Collaboration	
DSTC Pty Ltd

DSTC is looking at the technologies which enable much more flexible 
working arrangements than is possible in traditional organisations. We 
see social and technology trends such as: 

� increased numbers of telecommuters working from their homes 
� increased need for organisations to leverage the skills base of their 
workers regardless of worker location 
� increased need for collaboration across enterprise boundaries 
� increased connectivity within and between organisations allowing global 
availability of pay-as-you-use software and services 

DSTC's response to these changing trends is threefold: 

� We are building the infrastructure within our organisation to prepare 
ourselves for these oncoming trends. This includes deploying distributed 
file systems, email, news,
� web-based information and video conferencing between our sites - all of 
which allow greater inter-personal collaboration. In addition, we are 
deploying existing standards such as
� DCE which will allow secure sharing of software services across our 
sites. 
� We are starting on the process of extending this infrastructure to 
allow collaboration with our participants. This brings with it an 
additional level of complexity of issues related to
� different policies and work practices between the organisations. 
� We are conducting research to explore what applications will make use 
of emerging technologies and exploring what additional technologies are 
required to make deployment of
� these applications feasible.

Motorola (Hong Kong)	Motorola

Motorola (Hong Kong) is undertaking a distributed computing project in 
the Asia Pacific region.  The case study will focus on the changes to the 
organisational structure to put the new systems in place.  Motorola will 
implement five applications simultaneously in August 1995.  The company 
will be integrating heritage systems on MVS, migrating some to a UNIX 
solution with PC and Macintosh clients using object oriented technology 
at the desktop level.

System Integration Infrastructure (SII)	Optus


Single Point of Contact (SPOC) is an application used by Optus Customer 
Service Representatives to handle customer queries.  A key requirement of 
the application was the ability to access a large number of legacy 
applications and distributed heterogeneous back-end systems.  This ranged 
from mainframe applications to multi-vendor database systems to real-time 
switching equipment.  SPOC allows all of these information sources to be 
presented to the operator using a consistent user interface and more 
importantly a common view of customer.  The system is based on a decision 
support system which allows for easy configuration of business processes.

The SPOC application is built on top of a newly developed infrastructure 
developed by Digital Equipment Corporation called System Integration 
Infrastructure (SII).  SII incorporates the ability to access 
multi-vendor database systems, supports easy configuration of business 
processes, incorporates multiple facilities for accessing legacy 
applications, supports multiple mechanisms for inter-object communication 
including messaging systems, and provides for DCE-based security.

Telstra Network Operations Work Management System	Telstra

AIMS (Activity Information Management System) is a work management system 
developed for use in Telstra's Network Operations Business Unit. The 
system is intended to service some five thousand field staff located in 
centres around Australia.

Network Operations is primarily concerned with switching and transmission 
equipment located within network nodes. These sites largely have an 
existing infrastructure and Desktop Standard Operating Environment based 
on MS Windows PCs connected to TCP/IP and X.25 based Wide Area Networks. 

The requirement for AIMS is to present all work activities to field staff 
in a common and consistent way in a single Desktop application alongside 
other MS Windows applications. The system is required to route work to 
appropriate destinations based on defined business rules and to 
pro-actively manage the work to ensure completion on target.

AIMS is required to support 2,500 concurrent connections to Desktop PCs 
and handle some 18 million work items per year. The system has been 
implemented on four HP Unix machines.  Each of the two servers has an 
Oracle RDBMS back end. The four machines are configured in a DCE/Encina 
cell and are connected to several other Unix machines which interface to 
corporate mainframe hosts. 

The case study will present an overview of the design considerations for 
the system, in particular the use of DCE and Encina to achieve a large 
scale distributed computer system with high availability.

Other case studies include:

CiTR - Management in ATM Environments

Keynote Speakers

Ms Patricia Seybold is the president and CEO of the Patricia Seybold 
Group, a strategic technology consulting and information services firm 
located in Boston, Massachusetts. 

As a publisher, the Group produces dozens of targeted in-depth Research 
Reports describing and evaluating core technologies, such as Relational 
and Object Databases, Application Development Tools, Workflow Tools and 
Methodologies, and Document and Image Management Solutions.

In addition to tracking information technologies, Patricia�s particular 
areas of research interest are understanding the dynamics of the global 
information economy, tracking the evolution of the information 
marketplace, the design of knowledge-based organisations, and introducing 
the principles of individual and organisational learning into the design 
of information systems.

Ms Seybold will be discussing two topics; Enterprise Integration 
Incorporating New and Legacy Systems, and Business Process Re-engineering 
and Workflow.

Mr Bob Hayward is the Program Director of  the Gartner Group Asia Pacific 
Research Centre

Gartner Group, Inc. is a leading independent provider of 
subscription-based research and analysis of the IT industry. The 
Company's core business consists of research and analysis of significant 
IT industry  developments and trends, the packaging of such analyses into 
annually renewable subscription-based products called Continuous 
Services. Each Continuous Service product addresses a specific technology 
or market sector, and the Company's product offerings collectively 
provide comprehensive coverage of the IT industry.

Mr Hayward provides to Gartner Group members within Asia/Pacific, advice 
on networked systems management, open client/server systems, systems 
software (Unix and MVS), application development management, and analysis 
of markets and developments of the IT software industry.

His career in the IT industry spans 18 years. Mr Hayward joined Candle 
Corporation in Los Angeles in 1984 and led the development team which 
designed and developed �OMEGAMON for CICS/MVS�. He went on with Candle to 
head up European Technical support. In 1988, as Director of Asia/Pacific, 
Mr Hayward came to Australia to set up Candle�s Asia/Pacific 
headquarters. In 1992/93 he was Director of Distributed Systems with 
Candle in California.

Prior to joining Gartner Group Pacific, he ran his own distributorship 
and consultancy - Qualix Pty Ltd. Qualix has the distribution rights in 
Australia and New Zealand for Tivoli Systems Unix systems management 
software.

Mr Hayward�s presentation is titled, Distributed Computing - Future 
Trends in the Asia Pacific Region.

 
