> ==============================================================================
> Note 336.9                   Submission Invitation                      9 of 9
> TOPAZ::MORIN                                        5 lines  26-JUL-1995 11:55
>                       -< Referendum Ballots Being Mailed >-
> ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
>     The referendum ballot will be dropped at the Post Office tomorrow,
>     July 27 (on schedule).  Please note that the receipt deadline
>     for ballots to be counted is August 28, 1995.  

DECUS is running a special election to correct a technical problem with the
U.S. Chapter by-laws.  When the Chapter reorganized in December of 1993 it was
left with 2 of its 9 directors being accountable to a leadership group that no
longer existed (the Management Council).  The by-law change you are being
asked to vote on fixes this long standing problem.  After it is passed _all_
directors, with the exception of the Digital director, will be elected by the
membership. 

Please take the time to fill out and return the ballot.  This is a simple, and
I believe a change without controversy.  There in lies the problem.  With this
being the summer and the change being straight forward many may not feel the
need to vote.  Over the last 10 years we have seen even simple by-law changes
receive a number of no votes and therefore it is important you vote since
every no vote cancels 2 yes votes (2/3's+ required to pass). 

This is a single issue by-law change unlike past recent by-law changes.  All
it does is eliminates the no longer existing Management Council from the
by-laws and replace the Management Council elected directors with membership
elected directors.  Nothing else is changed.  The following is a copy of the
"why" statement that will be included with the ballots... 

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Statement in Support of the Bylaw Amendment

Background

The basic text of the Bylaws that currently govern the DECUS U.S. Chapter was 
put in place by vote of the membership in 1984 and has been relatively 
unchanged since.  These Bylaws set up a core organizational structure of a 
Board of Directors as the ultimate authority and a Management Council as the 
operations committee.  The Board of Directors continues to be composed of six 
members elected by the membership, one delegate selected by Digital 
Equipment, and the Chair and Vice-Chair of the Management Council. 

The Management Council was originally composed of representatives of the 
operational units (as the Bylaws terms them), the Executive Director of the 
Chapter, and a Chair and Vice-Chair, who are elected by the members of the 
Management Council and, therefore, ultimately accountable to the volunteer 
participants in the various operational units.  In 1993 the Management 
Council consisted of ten voting members.

In December of 1993, the Board of Directors reorganized the U.S. Chapter and, 
in the process, eliminated all operational units. This removed all members of 
the Management Council except the three specifically defined by the Bylaws 
(who represented no unit), the Executive Director, the Chair and the Vice-
Chair.  This left the Chair and Vice-Chair of the Management Council in the 
awkward position of having votes on the Board, but with their constituency 
removed.

In the spring of 1994, the Board of Directors proposed an amendment to the 
Bylaws that included a correction of this situation.  However, a number of 
unrelated, but complex and controversial changes were also included in that 
proposed amendment, and the proposal did not receive the votes necessary to 
pass.  The Management Council Chair and Vice-Chair remain Board members, even 
though the reorganization removed the group to which they were accountable. <

The Purpose of This Amendment

We propose, with this amendment, to remove all references to the Management 
Council from the Bylaws.  We also propose to expand the number of elected 
members of the Board of Directors by two, replacing the Management Council 
officer seats.  This keeps the size of the Board the same at nine voting 
members, eight elected by the Chapter membership and one appointed by 
Digital.  Elected Board members would continue to be elected for two year 
terms, with four (of eight) offices open for election each year.  The 
Executive Director remains, as before, a non-voting member of the Board.

Transition

If passed, the amendment will go into effect immediately.  This will create 
two vacancies on the Board of Directors, one with a term ending June 30, 1996 
and another with a term ending June 30, 1997.  The vacancies will be filled 
according to Section 6.9 of the Bylaws, which specifies appointment by the 
Chapter President with confirmation by the rest of the Board of Directors.  
These new directors will be accountable to the membership as are the other 
elected directors, and will stand for re-election by the membership at the 
end of their term.

Why Now

It is the usual practice for Bylaw amendments to be submitted to the 
membership in conjunction with the election for Board of Directors in the 
spring in order to reduce the overall cost of elections.  However, with the 
recent election of new Board members and a new slate of Chapter officers, the 
time is now for the building of a new team to move the Chapter forward.

Submitting this Bylaw amendment out of the normal election cycle also allows 
the new offices to be included in the next Board of Directors election.  If 
the amendment were passed in conjunction with that election, it would be more 
than a year before the first of the new offices could be filled by election.

The anomaly of the Management Council is unfinished business from the 
December 1993 reorganization.  The more than a year and a half it has been 
pending is too long.  The Chair and Vice-Chair of the Management Council join 
with all their fellow Board members in unanimously recommending a vote for 
this amendment.




