In article <1995Jul27.132352.15865@ludens>, mico@ludens.elte.hu writes:
>I want to install a DIGITAL
>
> DWCTX-BX TURBOchannel Adapter and a PMAD-AB Ethernet TURBOchannel Option Card 
>
>in a VAXstation 4000/60 running a VMS V5.5. 

Weird coincidence - I installed one just last night in a 4000/90.

>I followed the installation procedure. After the physical installation
>in console mode I entered the SHOW CONFIG command. In the list on the screen
>I found the line containing the unit number 13, named TCA. This seemed
>to be O.K. But for the SHOW DEVICE command I got a list containing only 
>the devices that had been installed before.

All this sounds just like what happened with my installation. I
was somewhat worried that only the turbochannel adapter and not
the ethernet card showed up in the show device listing, so I
looked up the troubleshooting section in the manual that came
with the hardware. This described something called the rex
emulator with which I could find the ethernet card and get it to
give me its ethernet address. I don't remember the details (I'm
at home - manual at work).

>When I tried to boot the VMS operating system, the boot procedure halted
>in the STARTUP command procedure at the SYSGEN AUTOCONFIGURE ALL command.
>The boot procedure here hung on such a way that even pressing the reset
>button of the workstation did not help, I had to turn power off and on
>again to reset. Finally I had to remove the newly installed boards to restore
>the original state. 

Mine booted right up - there were two new devices: ECA0: and
ECA1:, that I had not seen before.  I figured these were related
to the new ethernet card.  I went into NCP and tried to configure
ISB-0 - no luck.  So then I looked through netconfig.com and
found that the first EC device is configured as MXE-0. I used NCP
to configure line MXE-0 and circuit MXE and the show commands
look normal - including the ehternet hardware address.  I haven't
actually used the device for communication yet, as I've only had
a loopback connector plugged into it.

>If you have installed similar equipment or at least a
>second Ethernet adapter, or have any good idea, please share your 
>experiences/thoughts with me.

I have a vague recollection that I had to get to version 5.5-2 in
order to install the 4000/90.  Perhaps 5.5 is too early to
recognize this card.  You might look through netconfig.com and
see if it knows about EC devices and MXE devices.  Also, if you
don't have a good transceiver or loopback connector plugged in,
you might try that although I doubt this would be the problem.

					Ed


