> file services for our "front-office applications" (e.g. word processing)
> which are
> moving from VMS/VTxxx to WinTel workstations (Win95).  
> 
> There _will_ be a need for those staff remaining on VTxxx's to store/fetch
> files
> to/from the NT server and workstation users to store/fetch to/from VMS.
> File locking would be appropriate; i.e. I don't want two users editing the
> same
> file at the same time.
> 
> Can anyone tell me how file sharing is implemented in VMS and NT,
> respective of the other?

PATHWORKS for OpenVMS gives OpenVMS the capability to become a LANman server,
but not a client. If you want VT *and* Windows NT users to access a file,
keep it on OpenVMS.

With PATHWORKS, OpenVMS can become part of your Windows NT domain. We've got
one here, and it works fine. You can't tell whether your files are on
OpenVMS or another Windows NT server, and the files in the OpenVMS print
queues even show up in Print Manager.

