A simple reply:

	No, there is no difference.

A complicated reply:

	Since the Alpha instruction set does not have byte load and
	store from memory, the base machine is neither big-endian
	or little-endian.  The compilers for VMS create a little-
	endian behaviour (same as VAX).  I believe the compilers
	for OSF and WNT do the same, but I could be wrong.  I have
	not the faintest idea about the compiler for Linux.

	There is a slight bias in the Alpha instruction set toward
	little endian, as setting up for certain byte extraction
	operations takes the compiler an extra instruction if you
	want big-endian.  That instruction might be in an otherwise
	unused instruction slot anyway, so perhaps for your most
	critical inner loop there would be no difference.

Larry Kilgallen



