In article <KAGAN.95Jul8213243@athene.itep.ru>, kagan@athene.itep.ru (Roman Kagan) writes:
|	Hello DEC gurus!
|Recently we acquired several VAXstations 2000. They have rather poor
|CPU and memory, but quite good console monitors, so we'd like to run
|Xservers on them to connect to more powerful UNIX workstations. We
|have VMS 5.4 and MultiNet 3.2. What are our options?
|  Any help will be appreciated.

I'd run a minimal VMS (with the DECW server) on them, and run
a remote window manager on the Unix workstation.  You'd need to do 
something like the following (excerpted and adapted from Ed Groth's 
"Poor Man's X Terminal" article) for a VAXstation XSERV:

4. Allow access to the server from the client:

Create a file named DECW$SERVER_ACCESS_TRUSTED.DAT in
SYS$SPECIFIC:[SYSMGR] for node XSERV (and all other nodes which
you intend to be poor man's x terminals).  The file should contain
the line

TCPIP unixhost *

(transport node user)
(be sure to substitute your correct node name for unixhost)

5. Modify SYS$MANAGER:DECW$PRIVATE_APPS_SETUP.COM

Add the following to decw$private_apps_setup.com

$!
$! For nodes which are to be "poor man's x terminals", redefine the main
$! application.
$  pmxtermnodes="XSERV" !Be sure to substitute for XSERV
$  nodename=f$getsyi("NODENAME")
$  if f$locate(nodename,pmxtermnodes) .lt. f$length(pmxtermnodes)
$  then
$    decw$mainapp == "@sys$common:[pmxterm]pmxtermstart"
$  endif

If you have more than one node you want to be an X terminal, then use
$  pmxtermnodes="XSERV1/XSERV2/XSERV3" etc.

6. Create the new mainapp procedure.

In SYS$COMMON:[PMXTERM], place the following file named
pmxtermstart.com

$! This procedure is to be pointed at by decw$mainapps in
$! decw$private_apps_setup.com.
$! It is used to fire up a login process on a remote node
$! rather than the local node.
$ rsh unixhost start-window-session

Where, on unixhost, the "start-window-session" is a script
containing whatever commands are needed to get things displaying
back on XSERV1.

Something like this, anyway.

Cheers,

Aaron

