Brian Saunders (saunders@castor.che.wisc.edu) wrote:
: In article <3tno39$jil@agate.berkeley.edu>,
: peterm@landau.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) wrote:

: [swap horror story concerning RAM-intensive jobs deleted]

: >  Can anyone give some advice?

: I'd hate to say this, but "me too".  We see the same darned thing happen. 
: Our workaround is for people to watch the total memory usage, and not run
: a large job if another large job is running.

If you want to live dangerously ....
You can turn on lazy swap evaluation by renaming (or otherwise keeping
the system from seeing the name) /sbin/swapdefault .  This file is a link
to your swap device. Lazy evaluation causes no backing store to be allocated 
immediately for dynamic memory allocation (malloc) - it attempts to map the 
storage when required. Renaming the file  (you must also reboot after the 
renaming) may help you in this case. It may also cause "mysterious" failures 
in codes since malloc will always succeed.
Again, a dangerous method, but one which can sometimes boost performance
enough to make it worthwhile.
