vector@ids.com (Engineering) wrote:
>Actually, I think I know *what* it is, I just don't have a clue as to how
>to use it. Anybody with some knowledge of this program, please post or
>e-mail.
>
As you probably know, lzdcmp is a UNIX uncompress utility with a few
ehancements.

Define a symbol such as:

    $ uncompress :== $<path>lzdcmp

If you give it an invalid switch, like "$uncompress -h", it will give you:

The following options are valid:
-B      Binary file (important on VMS/RSX, ignored on Unix)
-F      Output fixed-block 512 byte records (VMS export only
-M val  Set the maximum number of code bits (unless header present)
-V val  Print status information or debugging data.
-X val  Set export (compatibility) mode:
-X 0    VMS private mode
-X 1    Compatibility with Unix compress
-X 2    Do not read a header, disable "block-compress" mode
        (If a header is present, lzdcmp will properly configure itself,
        overriding the -X, -B and -M flag values.

I usually include the "-X 2" in my symbol definition.

-Tom




