Hi,

I've had to install OPEN3D several times, on OSF/1 2.x and OSF/1 3.0.  When
installing OPEN3D, I always ensure that the X server isn't running.  This
way, the new X server is put into place; if it's still running, then the new
X server isn't activated.  Also, I use the GL test as a measure of the 
installation.  To run it, do this:

cd /usr/examples/GL/samples
./prim -auto

This goes through some graphics primitives; if the OPEN3D installation is
incorrect, this will fail miserably.  This does assume the the GL components
of the OPEN3D product set have been installed.

Good luck,

