Subject: Re: How to unlock DECwindows pause
Date: 1995/07/06
Message-ID: <1995Jul5.163014.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 105617148
sender: news@unixhub.SLAC.Stanford.EDU
references: <3tf68k$54a@st-james.comp.vuw.ac.nz>
organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <3tf68k$54a@st-james.comp.vuw.ac.nz>, 
    					srlnrow@alpha.gns.cri.nz writes:
> What is the cleanest way of interrupting a DECwindows session that a user
> has put into the "paused" state (ie, locked with the screen displaying
> a password prompt). This is DECwindows on OpenVMS AXP 1.5.
> 
> I know that if I issue a STOP/ID on the Session Manager process, that will
> unlock the display, but it also means that the user's Session Manager is gone.
> Is there another (sub)process I should look for? Under Unix (Solaris 2.x), I
> can simply send a SIGHUP to the the xlock process to force it to release the
> display. I'm looking to do a similar thing on our Alpha.
> 
> NB: Using AUTHORIZE to change the user's password is not an optimal answer!

        Pardon me for being dense, but why would you want to do anything
    _other_ than kill the user's entire  session?   If there is a way to
    abort  the  pause  screen, but leave the  user's  applications  (and
    window manager) still  running,  I  would  consider  that  a  severe
    security hole.

        Having never done STOP/ID= on a DECW$SESSION, is there a problem
    like left-over applications/windows  that  didn't terminate when the
    session  was  stopped?  If that's the problem, I suggest  restarting
    the DECwindows server.  From a privileged account  on  the  node  in
    question, do

        $ @SYS$MANAGER:DECW$STARTUP RESTART

    This  will  kill  the  current  DECwindows  server  and  all running
    applications,    restart   the   server   (without   rebooting   the
    workstation), and present a fresh login screen at the console.

        -Ken
