(Once again, I don't have hardware documentation on this machine. Bear w/ me. :)

I've got a VAXstation 3100/30 that I'm trying to replace the system disk in.
The system disk was once an RZ23 (100 meg); it's currently a Seagate ST1480N
(406 meg).  Now I'm trying to move to a Maxtor MXT-540SL (540 meg), and I'm
having problems.

Before, all I needed to do to transfer things was a "backup/image" from the
old disk to the new disk, and all was well.  This time, the new disk won't
boot, no matter what contortions I go through....  All I get is:

>>> b dka400:
-DKA400
?43 FILESTRUCT
 84 FAIL
>>>

...yet if I boot from the old system disk, I can mount the disk and copy files
to it for days with no problems!  Analyze/disk/repair finds nothing wrong,
either.  I've tried a bunch of things, such as booting from a VMS 6.2 CD and
trying to install, doing 'init' by hand and copying things over, running
STABACIT to install just a standalone backup boot on it, and every time, the
filesystem gets built just fine...  it just can't boot from it.

The only thing I can think of (and this is so MS-DOS-like I'm afraid to say it)
is that maybe in addition to the 1 gig limit on boot disks, is there is also
a 64 sector per track limit?  The Maxtor's geometry exceeds that:
1754 cylinders, 7 heads, 87 sectors (1067740 blocks), while the Seagate
I'm using now is 1446 cylinders, 9 heads, 64 sectors (832527 blocks).
The drive is low-level formatted w/ 512 byte sectors (I tried it on a 
NeXT and it verified this, but I low level formatted it again just in
case; didn't make any difference.)

Anyone got an idea of what I'm leaving out (including any details I've
left out that you'd need to answer me? :)

