>: Our workaround is for people to watch the total memory usage, and not run
>: a large job if another large job is running.

And me as well.

>If you want to live dangerously ....
>You can turn on lazy swap evaluation by renaming (or otherwise keeping
>the system from seeing the name) /sbin/swapdefault .  This file is a link

We gave up on the default mode a long time ago.  We run several huge processes 
on alphas with 128 meg of memory.  We keep track of swap space using vmstat 
and ultimately the kernel warning messages.  Whenever we are getting low on 
swap space we start killing off nonessential processes.  OSF is singularly 
unforgiving about who it kills off if you let overcommitment go too far.  One 
time everything stayed up but inetd!

I'd like to hear from anyone who has been able to tune thier system well 
enough to use immediate mode with large processes running.

